The evidence supports only that repeated calls to the same model produced different reply forms, and that the larger replacement model did too. It does not by itself support the conclusion that the first model was unreliable or that a larger model from the same family would fix the issue. The inference may be wrong because two calls are a very small sample; different surface forms may still be semantically equivalent; no decoding settings, seeds, temperature, error rates, or task-scoring rules are reported; and similar variation in the larger model suggests a shared cause such as sampling nondeterminism, evaluation instability, or formatting variation rather than model capacity.